 ## Architecture
 
-Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
+Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed, independent of how records are stored or queried. The certificates node uses only this interface.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, index preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. Lookup is served from a local embedded SQLite index kept on kiosk storage. The USB snapshot carries the signed newline-delimited records; each kiosk builds its own index from them on import, so no index is transported between machines.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. These checks run against the snapshot records before any index is built; a snapshot that fails any of them is rejected and never reaches the build step.
+
+Build the SQLite index for a validated snapshot into a staging location separate from the active index. The build writes every record and stores the snapshot date as index metadata. Activation is a single atomic replacement of the active index by the staged one, so a lookup ever sees either the whole previous snapshot or the whole new one — never a partially built index. A build or activation failure leaves the previous active index in place and reports a useful error.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters in the entered serial before querying; serial-number punctuation remains significant. Query the active SQLite index by the normalized serial on an indexed column and return `Found(record, snapshotDate)` on a hit or `NotFound(snapshotDate)` on a miss. The snapshot date is read from index metadata and returned on both outcomes.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index on kiosk storage | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Both networked alternatives need the lab network at lookup time, which the HLD's offline tenets forbid; a local index is the only candidate that serves lookups entirely from kiosk files. Within that, SQLite gives an indexed lookup over 90,000 records inside the 250 ms / 4 GB goal without an in-memory scan of the full snapshot. |
+| Index provenance | Each kiosk builds its index from the snapshot records on import | Transport a prebuilt index on the USB media | The signature covers the records; rebuilding locally keeps the trusted artifact the signed snapshot rather than a derived index, at the cost of per-kiosk build work on import. |
+| Activation | Build to a staging location, then atomically replace the active index | Write records into the live index in place | In-place writes expose a partially imported snapshot and cannot leave the previous snapshot intact on failure; staged replacement satisfies both. |
+
